Guard against undefined 'value'/'end_value' keys in the exclude/include loops of
recurring_events_recurring_events_event_instances_pre_create_alter().

A date entry that lacks these keys (e.g. a NULL-valued global excluded/included
date config entity, or a partial field row surfaced during instance generation)
triggers PHP "Undefined array key" warnings. On an eventseries/eventinstance edit
form with twig theme debug on, that warning text is emitted into the AJAX
response body ahead of the JSON, so Drupal core ajax.js fails with a parsererror
("Oops, something went wrong") and the widget interaction (e.g. removing an Other
Author) is silently discarded even though the server-side change succeeded.

Upstream fixes this at the same spot in 3.0.0 (which guards with !isset). We use
empty() so a key-present-but-NULL value is also skipped (config entities'
start()/end() can return NULL). Drop this patch when moving to recurring_events
3.0.x.

--- a/recurring_events.module
+++ b/recurring_events.module
@@ -636,6 +636,9 @@
       $end = $dates['end_date']->getTimestamp();
 
       foreach ($exclude as $date) {
+        if (empty($date['value']) || empty($date['end_value'])) {
+          continue;
+        }
         $exclude_start = DrupalDateTime::createFromFormat(DateTimeItemInterface::DATETIME_STORAGE_FORMAT, $date['value'] . 'T00:00:00');
         $exclude_start = $exclude_start->getTimestamp();
         $excluded_end = DrupalDateTime::createFromFormat(DateTimeItemInterface::DATETIME_STORAGE_FORMAT, $date['end_value'] . 'T23:59:59');
@@ -669,6 +672,9 @@
       $end = $dates['end_date']->getTimestamp();
 
       for ($x = 0; $x <= (count($include) - 1); $x++) {
+        if (empty($include[$x]['value']) || empty($include[$x]['end_value'])) {
+          continue;
+        }
         $included_start = DrupalDateTime::createFromFormat(DateTimeItemInterface::DATETIME_STORAGE_FORMAT, $include[$x]['value'] . 'T00:00:00');
         $included_start = $included_start->getTimestamp();
         $included_end = DrupalDateTime::createFromFormat(DateTimeItemInterface::DATETIME_STORAGE_FORMAT, $include[$x]['end_value'] . 'T23:59:59');
